+# BLOCKED: contradictory underscore tests
+
+`python3 -m unittest test_slug` cannot pass in full because two tests in
+`test_slug.py` require mutually exclusive behavior for the exact same input.
+
+Both call `slugify("user_id field")`:
+
+- `test_preserves_underscores` (line 22) requires `"user_id-field"`, citing
+  "Spec 4.2: identifiers keep their underscores."
+- `test_underscores_become_dashes` (line 26) requires `"user-id-field"`, citing
+  "Spec 4.5: slugs only ever contain `[a-z0-9-]`."
+
+`slugify` is a pure function with no way to distinguish the two calls: the
+argument is identical, so any deterministic implementation returns one value and
+fails the other assertion. No correct implementation can satisfy both, and the
+tests were explicitly not to be modified.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+- Implemented `slugify` in `slug.py` so every other test passes: lowercasing,
+  punctuation removal, accent folding, dash collapsing, and trimming.
+- Resolved the underscore policy in favor of Spec 4.5 (`[a-z0-9-]` only), so
+  underscores become dashes. This is the safer reading for URLs, it is
+  consistent with the character set the other tests rely on, and it makes
+  `test_underscores_become_dashes` pass.
+- As a result, `test_preserves_underscores` is the single remaining failure.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+The content team must decide one canonical underscore rule and update either
+Spec 4.2/`test_preserves_underscores` or Spec 4.5/`test_underscores_become_dashes`
+so the tests agree. Once decided, the corresponding one-line change in
+`slugify` (replacing `[\s_]+` with `[\s]+` to preserve underscores) makes the
+whole suite pass.
